## Research Question #1

- Do rural fatal crashes involve more fatalities per crash than urban fatal crashes, and how are crash, road, and driver characteristics related to this difference?

### Hypotheses

- Null hypothesis: There is no difference in the number of fatalities per crash between rural and urban crashes. Crash, roadway, and driver characteristics are not associated with the number of fatalities per crash.
- Alternative hypothesis: Rural fatal crashes involve a greater number of fatalities per crash than urban fatal crashes. Crash, roadway, and driver characteristics are associated with the number of fatalities per crash.

### Variables

- Independent: Geographic location (RUR_URB)
- Dependent: Number of fatalities (FATALS)

### Key Predictors

- RUR_URB, speed limit (VSPD_LIM), weather, lighting (LGT_COND), time of day (HOUR), driver age, alcohol (DRINKING), travel speed (TRAV_SP), rollover, road classification (FUNC_SYS), speed‑related indicator (SPEEDREL).

### Methods

- Two‑sample t-test or similar for mean fatalities rural vs urban
- Multiple or negative binomial regression with these predictors to model the number of fatalities per fatal crash.

### Prepping data for question 1

```{r}
## summarize vehicle level data to one observation per crash (ST_CASE)
## there are a lot of missing travel speed values (over half of observations) which creates an issue
vehicle_summary <- vehicle |>
  mutate(
    TRAV_SP = ifelse(
      TRAV_SPNAME %in% c(
        "Not Reported",
        "Reported as Unknown", 
        "Stopped Motor Vehicle In-Transport"), 
      NA, 
      TRAV_SP
    ) #replace travel speed codes with NA if their description is not reported, reported as unknown, or stopped motor vehicle in-transport 
    ) |>
  group_by(ST_CASE) |>
  summarise(
    max_travel_speed = max(TRAV_SP, na.rm = TRUE),
    any_rollover = any(ROLLOVERNAME == "Rollover"),
    any_speed_related = any(SPEEDRELNAME %in% c(
      "Yes, Exceeded Speed Limit",
      "Yes, Too Fast for Conditions",
      "Yes, Racing",
      "Yes, Specifics Unknown")
    )
  )
#This summarizes each ST_CASE with a max travel speed, rollover classification, and speed-related classification. The max travel speed returns a real value or a -inf for those with NA

vehicle_summary <- vehicle_summary |>
  mutate(
    max_travel_speed = ifelse(is.infinite(max_travel_speed), NA, max_travel_speed))
# This replaces the -inf values with NA 

vehicle_summary <- vehicle_summary |>
  mutate(
    max_travel_speed = ifelse(max_travel_speed == 997, NA_real_, max_travel_speed))
# replaces special code 997 (Greater then 151 mph) with NA 
```

```{r}
## summarize person level data to one observation per crash (ST_CASE)
## create variables for youngest and oldest driver in each crash to account for crashes involving multilpe drivers 
driver_summary <- person |>
  mutate(
    # replacing unknown or unreported driver ages with NA
    AGE = ifelse(AGE >= 998, NA, AGE)
  ) |>
  filter(
    PER_TYPNAME == "Driver of a Motor Vehicle In-Transport") |>
  group_by(ST_CASE) |>
  summarise(
    youngest_driver_age = min(AGE, na.rm = TRUE),
    oldest_driver_age = max(AGE, na.rm = TRUE),
    any_drinking = any(DRINKINGNAME == "Yes (Alcohol Involved)", na.rm = TRUE)
  )
# instead of filtering out the weird ages I assign NA so we can keep data for the other variables like drinking 

driver_summary <- driver_summary |>
  mutate(
    youngest_driver_age = ifelse(is.infinite(youngest_driver_age), NA, youngest_driver_age),
    oldest_driver_age = ifelse(is.infinite(oldest_driver_age), NA, oldest_driver_age))
# using the same code from vehicle chunk we have to replace the inf- from summarizing the NAs with a new NA value 
```


```{r}
## join summarized data from person and vehicle into accident based on ST_CASE
question1_data <- accident |>
  select(
    ST_CASE,
    RUR_URB,
    RUR_URBNAME,
    FATALS,
    WEATHER,
    WEATHERNAME,
    LGT_COND,
    LGT_CONDNAME,
    HOUR,
    FUNC_SYS,
    FUNC_SYSNAME
  ) |>
  left_join(vehicle_summary, by = "ST_CASE") |>
  left_join(driver_summary, by = "ST_CASE") 
```

